are that's been a government tactic  in recent weeks cutting off mobile  internet to prevent  footage of all of this getting out but  you don't get a sense from people here  that they're really scared the police we  have some  police officers getting up with  megaphones telling people reminding them  that these protests were illegal that  taking part in these protests uh was an  illegal act and they were booed out you  didn't get any sense that anyone was  particularly scared of them  although protesters are sure that there  are um security service people in  civilian  clothes filming faces going through the  crowds trying to work out who  uh who is coordinating all of this but  it's that's the problem for the regime  right now these are mass protests that  seem to be  uncoordinated that are happening  spontaneously so it's not easy for the  regime to crack down on people  another interesting detail um we've seen  restrictions on access to mints today  roads  leading to the capital being either shut  or being restricted to  speeds documents being checked  unnecessarily as a way of trying to pull  down the count out  to become the turnout rather for this  demonstration but it doesn't seem to  have worked this is  a huge demonstration of something that  frankly would have been unthinkable in  belarus just a few months ago  nick spontaneous but certainly massive  we're seeing images of  just huge gatherings of what appear to  be pretty resolute people standing their  ground there the belarus's defense  minister viktor krenim  says authorities will deploy inments to  protect  monuments implying of course that they  would potentially use  the army against these demonstrators it  sounds to me nick as if the government's  tactic is to show a strong hand rather  than to comet citizens  well quite and this seems for now to  being counterproductive people are  outraged  the ones i've spoken to who've been  reading this news in the recent minutes  that this news only came out 